# Project: Locust Python Load Testing Framework

## Project Structure
- Organise locustfiles in a `locustfiles/` directory grouped by feature or service
- Place shared utilities, tasks, and data helpers under `locustfiles/common/`
- Use a single entry point `locustfile.py` at the project root for simple setups
- Store environment-specific configuration in `config/` as YAML or `.env` files

## Coding Standards
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines; enforce with ruff or flake8
- Use type hints for all task functions and helper methods
- Name task methods descriptively: `browse_product_catalogue`, `submit_checkout`
- Keep individual task functions short and focused on one user action

## User Definition
- Subclass `HttpUser` (or `FastHttpUser` for high-throughput scenarios) for HTTP load tests
- Define `wait_time` using `between(min, max)` for realistic think-time simulation
- Override `on_start` for per-user setup (login, token retrieval) and `on_stop` for teardown
- Group related tasks into `TaskSet` classes and reference them via `tasks` attribute

## Task Organisation
- Use `@task(weight)` to reflect realistic traffic distribution across endpoints
- Break complex flows into reusable helper methods called from task functions
- Use `SequentialTaskSet` for ordered user journeys that must execute step-by-step
- Avoid shared mutable state between users; use instance variables on the `User` class

## Load Shapes
- Define custom load profiles by subclassing `LoadTestShape` and overriding `tick()`
- Implement ramp-up, steady-state, and ramp-down phases in the shape class
- Use `StepLoadShape` for staircase load patterns without custom code
- Validate that the shape reflects production traffic patterns, not arbitrary spikes

## Best Practices
- Use `self.client.get/post` with `name=` parameter to group dynamic URLs in statistics
- Always assert response status with `response.raise_for_status()` or explicit checks
- Use `catch_response=True` context manager to mark failures on business-level checks
- Avoid `time.sleep()` inside tasks; rely on `wait_time` for think-time simulation
- Parameterise test data; never hardcode credentials or user-specific values in tasks

## Data Management
- Load test data from CSV or JSON files at module level using `locust.contrib.fasthttp`
- Use `itertools.cycle` or a queue to distribute data across virtual users without repeats
- Generate dynamic data with `faker` for fields that must be unique per request
- Store sensitive credentials in environment variables, never in committed files

## Distributed Execution
- Run in distributed mode with one master and multiple worker processes: `locust --master` / `locust --worker`
- Size workers based on available CPU cores; one worker per core is a reasonable baseline
- Use `--expect-workers` on the master to delay test start until all workers connect
- Deploy workers in containers (Docker / Kubernetes) for scalable cloud load generation

## Metrics and Thresholds
- Define pass/fail criteria programmatically in a `LoadTestShape` or via `--exit-code-on-error`
- Track `response_times.percentile(95)` and `response_times.percentile(99)` for SLO validation
- Monitor failure rate with `stats.total.fail_ratio`; fail the run if it exceeds the threshold
- Export results with `--csv=results` for post-run trend analysis

## Reporting
- Use the built-in web UI (`--web-host`, `--web-port`) for real-time monitoring during runs
- Generate HTML reports with `--html=report.html` for archiving and sharing results
- Integrate with Grafana and InfluxDB via the `locust-influxdb-listener` plugin for dashboards
- Store CSV output in CI artefacts for historical comparison across builds

## CI/CD Integration
- Run headless with `--headless -u <users> -r <spawn-rate> --run-time <duration>`
- Set `--exit-code-on-error 1` so CI fails when Locust detects request failures
- Separate smoke tests (low VU count, short duration) from full load tests in pipeline stages
- Cache the virtual environment between runs to reduce pipeline startup time

## Environment Configuration
- Use environment variables or a config file to switch between test, staging, and production targets
- Pass the target host via `--host` at runtime; never hardcode URLs inside locustfiles
- Use `locust.env.host` inside tasks to construct endpoint URLs dynamically
- Manage secrets with a vault tool (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) in CI pipelines

## Error Handling
- Catch `ConnectionError` and `Timeout` exceptions in tasks and report them via `request_failure` event
- Use `response.failure("reason")` inside `catch_response` blocks for application-level errors
- Log unexpected failures with the Locust logger: `from locust import log; log.info(...)`
- Implement retry logic only for idempotent GET requests and with a fixed maximum retry count

## Documentation
- Document each `User` class and `TaskSet` with a brief docstring describing the simulated persona
- Maintain a `README.md` in `locustfiles/` explaining how to run, configure, and extend tests
- Record baseline performance numbers and SLO targets alongside the locustfile they validate
- Include example `docker-compose.yml` for reproducing distributed runs locally

Remember to model realistic user behaviour and validate results against defined SLOs rather than chasing raw throughput numbers.
